course: dictionary
lesson: 13794
title: Words that push in
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Invasive means something pushes into a place where it does not belong. The idea stays the same, but the subject changes it a little. Look at the words around it for clues. «The cane toad is invasive in Australia, where it was never meant to be.» The clue is "where it was never meant to be", so invasive is about a plant or animal.
explain: Now read this: «The doctor said the test was non-invasive, so there was no cutting.» The start non- means "not", so non-invasive means the test does not go inside the body. Swap in your meaning to check: "the test did not go inside the body" still makes sense.
explain: «Her friend asked invasive questions about her private life.» Nobody cut anything here. Invasive questions go too far into someone's private life. Ask yourself: is this pushing into something that should stay private?
fact: Invasive means pushing into a place, or a body, where something does not belong.
fact: An invasive plant or animal spreads and harms the plants and animals already living there.
fact: Cane toads are an invasive species in Australia, and grey squirrels are invasive in the UK, where they push out red squirrels.
fact: Japanese knotweed is an invasive plant that spreads fast and can damage walls and paths.
fact: «Non-invasive» means not going inside the body, as in a «non-invasive test».
exercise: «The cane toad is invasive in Australia.» What does invasive suggest here? | Very small and quiet | Found only in a zoo | *Spreads where it does not belong and harms the animals already there | Friendly to all the local animals
exercise: «The doctor's test was non-invasive, with no cutting.» What does non-invasive mean? | Happens only in the dark | Needs a long wait | *Does not go inside the body | Goes deep inside the body
exercise: «She said the questions were invasive and too personal.» What does invasive mean here? | Asking very quietly | *Going too far into someone's private life | Asking in a kind way that helps | Asking about the weather
test: «The invasive plant covered the pond in a week.» Find and copy the word that means spreads where it does not belong. | plant | *invasive | pond | covered
test: «The cancer was invasive, spreading into other parts of the body.» What does invasive mean here? | Gone away for good | Very small and hard to see | *Spreading into other parts of the body | Gentle and slow
exam: Ravi says invasive means "very loud". Is he right? | Yes, it means very loud | Yes, it means very kind | No, it means very quick | *No, it means pushing into a place, or a body, where it does not belong
